# Copy to `.env` beside docker-compose.yml and edit. `.env` is the
# operator's file: it is not in this repository and must not be added to it.
#
# No secret is written here — only the *path* to one (invariant 7).

# Which hub image this deployment runs. Required: `docker-compose.yml`
# refuses to start without it rather than guessing, because the wrong hub
# starting quietly is worse than a compose file that stops.
#
# The value is the tag `docker load` printed when you loaded the archive
# attached to the GitHub release (D162):
#
#   sha256sum --ignore-missing -c SHA256SUMS
#   docker load < herd-hub-image-0.3.3.tar.gz   # Loaded image: herd-hub:0.3.3
#
#   HERD_HUB_IMAGE=herd-hub:0.3.3
#
# Read the tag `docker load` prints rather than copying the number above: it
# is the workspace's own version (`nix/hub-image.nix`), and this file is not
# re-dated every release.
#
# That tag is **local to the machine that loaded it**. There is no registry
# behind it, so `docker compose pull` fails on it — skip the pull and go
# straight to `up -d`. `README.md` walks the first start.
#
# **Never `latest`, and there is none to name.** A floating tag means the
# next `docker compose up -d` can bring the hub back on a different
# `PROTOCOL_VERSION`, and a protocol move is a fleet-wide restart of every
# runner and every `herdctl` at once, not a container coming back
# (`release-notice.md`). Pin the version, read the notice, move the fleet.
# A loaded tag is pinned by construction: nothing can move it but another
# `docker load`.
#
# Building the archive locally instead of downloading it — `nix build
# .#hub-image` and `docker load`, which `README.md` documents — is the same
# derivation and gives the same tag. And if you have put the image in a
# registry of your own (`flake.nix`, the `release` shell, says how in one
# command), any reference docker can pull is accepted here, a digest
# included.
HERD_HUB_IMAGE=herd-hub:0.3.3

# The file holding the one shared token every requester must carry (Q8).
# Make it like this, outside any repository:
#
#   install -d -m 0700 /etc/herd
#   head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 > /etc/herd/requester-token
#   chmod 0444 /etc/herd/requester-token
#
# The mode is deliberate, and 0444 is not a mistake. Docker bind-mounts this
# file into the container as it finds it: the `uid`, `gid` and `mode` a
# compose secret may declare are honoured by swarm and *silently ignored*
# here, so the hub — uid 1000, never root — reads it with exactly the host's
# ownership and mode. Left `0400` and root-owned, as a secret ought to be,
# the first start dies with `reading the requester token from
# /run/secrets/herd-requester-token: Permission denied (os error 13)`.
#
# What keeps the token private on the host is therefore the *directory*, not
# the file: `/etc/herd` is 0700 and root's, so no unprivileged user on the
# host can reach the path at all. Inside the container 0444 means "the hub
# and root", because those are the only two users in the image.
#
# The *runner's* token beside it, `/etc/herd/runner-token.env` in
# docs/joining.md, is 0400 and stays 0400: systemd reads an
# `EnvironmentFile=` as root before it drops to `User=herd`, so that one
# never needs opening up. Same directory, two mechanisms, two modes.
#
# Requesters then carry it as `herdctl --token` or HERD_TOKEN.
HERD_REQUESTER_TOKEN_FILE=/etc/herd/requester-token
